From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lewis Gibson <12851394+lewisgibson@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 18:24:32 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add save-all [flush] for region threading

Folia did not register /save-all, so scripts that bracket a file copy with
"save-off", "save-all flush" and "save-on" got "Unknown command" and copied
region files that were still being written. Register the command again.

The invariant: when the command reports "Saved the game" after "save-all
flush", every chunk, entity chunk, poi chunk and player that was loaded and
modified before the command was issued has been serialised and written to its
region file, and the region files have been forced to disk. A failure that the
server only logs while saving, such as a player file that cannot be written or a
chunk that fails to serialise, does not turn the reply into a failure, as in
Paper. save-off stops the autosave, chunk unloads and player disconnects still
write, as in Paper.

How it holds:
- Serialising a chunk or a player must be done by the thread that owns it, so
  RegionizedSaveAll queues one task per region section (RegionizedTaskQueue
  moves the task correctly if the region merges or splits before it runs, and
  the section list is taken atomically under the regioniser lock) and one task
  per player on its entity scheduler.
- The waiting is done on a dedicated non tick thread. Nothing here blocks a tick
  thread, so it cannot deadlock a single tick thread configuration on its own
  region tasks. Level data and the global saved data are written by a task on
  the global region, which owns them.
- MoonriseRegionFileIO#flush waits for the I/O queue to be empty, which on a
  running server may never happen because reads and new saves keep arriving.
  flushPendingWrites waits for the chunks that had a write pending when it was
  called, then forces the region files. A later write to one of those chunks
  reuses its task and is waited on too.
- The command replies from the worker thread. An rcon client is answered when
  runCommand returns, so RconConsoleSource lets a command register a pending
  completion and runCommand waits for it before it reads the response. The rcon
  reply is the same as vanilla: "Saving the game (this may take a moment!)Saved
  the game".
- ServerLevel#noSave is volatile, as it is now written by the global region and
  read by every region tick, and the autosave of level data and global saved
  data on the global region honours it, as vanilla's autosave does.

